ook-pencil-shipment (the source has enough boxed pencils: was 0, needed 1; business.packed_pencils is raised only by pack-pencils (on its actor)); buy-pencil (the shop has a pencil in stock: was 0, needed 1; nothing ever raises shop.packed_pencils); deliver-pencil-shipment (the shipment has cargo: was 0, needed 0; shipment.cargo is raised only by book-pencil-shipment (on its actor), each on the one who does it, so no action ever brings shipment.cargo to this actor; the shipment has a destination: was None, needed None; shipment.destination is raised only by book-pencil-shipment (on its actor), each on the one who does it, so no action ever brings shipment.destination to this actor); make-leads (the lead works has enough graphite: was 0, needed 1; business.graphite is raised only by mine-graphite (on its actor), each on the one who does it, so no action ever brings business.graphite to this actor); pack-pencils (the factory has enough finished pencils: was 0, needed 1; business.pencils is raised only by assemble-pencils (on its actor), each on the one who does it, so no action ever brings business.pencils to this actor); saw-slats (the sawmill has enough logs: was 0, needed 1; business.logs is raised only by cut-cedar (on its actor), each on the one who does it, so no action ever brings business.logs to this actor). For each, make sure some other action produces what its checks need (for example, if goods must be at the next stop before they can move on, give someone an action that brings them there). Nothing in the world ever makes or brings in these, and everyone starts with none, so every step that uses them is impossible: packed_pencils. Give each one a source: an actor whose action makes or supplies it, or a process that renews it at the place it comes from. These goods are made or supplied but no move ever takes them from where they pile up, so the step that needs them never gets them: graphite piles up at graphite-mine (mine-graphite); leads piles up at lead-works (make-leads); log",
  "authority_rules": [
    "Every move and process must keep the world's laws: a resource with a total law changes in total only through the moves listed in may_change_by and through processes whose process_id is listed there as 'process:<process_id>'; every other effect on it subtracts from one holder exactly what it adds to another. A pairing law's move hands over its 'gives' and its 'pays' in the same move, with a check that each giver has enough. A process the laws name as 'process:<id>' must have exactly that process_id. The engine refuses any step that breaks a law. When there are no laws, this rule says nothing.",
    "Use only action participants as effect targets.",
    "Go through the represented things and moves for consequences that follow physically at once (a spill wets what is under it, a loud change such as an engine starting is heard by people in the same place) and write each one the represented state can hold as a process with timing immediately.",
    "Every effect must be able to change state: never add or subtract 0 and never set a field to its own value. If a move has no represented field it would change, set a stage or add 1 to a count that the description implies, or say in limits what is left out.",
    "When a move exchanges something for a price and both sides have a represented amount of money, the same move subtracts the price from the payer and adds it to the payee, with a check that the payer has at least the price. Effects cannot multiply, so a priced move hands over exactly one unit (add and subtract the literal 1, ignoring any quantity field) and charges the price once; selling more takes more moves. (2026-10-26 pencil build NZD33sh3iX29: a sale of several pencils charged for one.)",
    "A process acts on each matching entity `it`. When one physical change moves or changes a linked entity too (a driver riding in a vehicle, a hospital whose bed a dying patient frees), declare that entity in the process's participants (link owner_of_it, owned_by_it, co_located, or ref:<component>.<field>) and target it by that name in the same process; never split one physical change across two processes.",
    "When a consequence follows physically at once from another change (something unsupported falls, a fragile thing that fell breaks, a broken container spills, a breaking thing makes a sound), make it a process with timing immediately whose checks name the state that change leaves; each step of a chain is its own process. Keep timing each_round for what takes time (drying, growing, travelling, fuel).",
    "Fields such as upright, full, spilled, broken and wet, with an entity_ref 'on' naming what a thing rests on, already state the physics; using them is not an assumption. When a world has them, write the ordinary consequences as processes with timing immediately: a full container that is no longer upright spills (full false, spilled true); a spill wets what its 'on' names (participant linked by ref:<component>.on); a fragile thing that falls breaks. Keep the move itself to its direct change.",
    "When an entity has an integer field counting rounds left (a delivery on the road, a journey, a job under way, a deadline), the action that starts it sets that count and does not finish it at once; add a process with timing each_round that lowers the count by 1 while it is above 0, and make the arrival or completion (goods reaching their destination, the job done) depend on the count being 0.",
    "Checks on location/ownership/portable paths must agree with initial_builtin_state (null means unset), so that at least one action is possible from the starting state.",
    "Never check ownership.owner_ref or portable.portable on an entity whose initial_builtin_state value is null, unless one of your own effects sets that value first; use component fields instead.",
    "A participant's selector must list in `components` every component whose fields that participant's checks or effects read or write.",
    "When entities start in different places (initial_builtin_state location values differ), an action that uses, takes, operates or talks to a thing or person must check that the actor's location.location_id equals that participant's location.location_id.",
    "Except: an order or purchase between parties in different places is sent from afar. Give it no same-place check unless one of your moves or processes can bring them together; when the goods travel, start a delivery that counts down rounds instead.",
    "Every check must hold at the same time; there is no 'or'. Never require one value to equal two different fields. For a way between two places, require the actor's location to equal the way's first place field and the destination to equal its second, and name the reverse direction in limits.",
    "Checks and effects must use only allowed_state_paths.",
    "Do not invent source code, hidden state, or new entities.",
    "An action should change only state causally implied by its description and represented world.",
    "Use owner_ref expressions for actor ownership rather than hard-coded actor ids. {owner_ref: P} is the string 'actor:<id of P>', meaning 'owned by P'; it is NOT the owner of P. To require that thing X belongs to the acting actor, compare {participant: {name: X, path: ownership.owner_ref}} with {owner_ref: actor}. Never put {owner_ref: X} for a thing X on the left of a check (that would ask whether X owns itself).",
    "Terminal, if present, must be derivable from represented state and must not duplicate a completion flag.",
    "Limits and tests must name omissions and refusal/boundary cases honestly.",
    "Every check label is a short plain-English condition a visitor understands, using entity labels (e.g. \"the watering can is free\", \"Ava is holding the knife\"); never jargon such as actor, owner_ref, context, entity, component or participant.",
    "World kind: keeps running. terminal must be null. Add 1-4 processes so the world never runs down. If the description is about work or production: keep raw materials renewing at their source and demand arriving (raw materials regrow or are mined each round, customers want more, a served order becomes a new waiting order), so goods keep flowing through the chain and every actor role has something useful to do in most rounds, forever. A process never adds a good that an action makes from other goods (boards, parts, finished products): those come only from that action. A process never moves goods from one holder to another: carrying is an actor's action. A raw material has one source: if an actor's action gathers it (mining, cutting), that action takes it from a reserve and the process renews the reserve, not the gatherer's stock. Each actor's actions should be limited to its own step by checks on its role, and moving goods should add to the next holder's stock and subtract from the previous one's. If it is about people living their lives: change needs and resources every round in a cycle (for example hunger rises AND food regrows, supplies run down AND are restocked), never only decay, so they keep acting on their own needs with no goal imposed."
  ],
